{"schema_version":"1.9.2","id":"GHSA-rq8q-p985-fjq7","published":"2026-11-12T23:39:41Z","modified":"2026-11-12T23:45:03.332653910Z","aliases":["CVE-2026-126070"],"summary":"Action View: Possible XSS vulnerability in strip_tags","details":"### Impact\n`strip_tags` in Action View returns its sanitized output marked `html_safe`, but the underlying sanitizer encodes only `&`, `<` and `>`, leaving `\"` and `'` unencoded. Because ERB does not escape a string marked `html_safe`, untrusted input passed through `strip_tags` and rendered inside a quoted HTML attribute value could close the attribute and add new attributes, possibly leading to XSS. Applications that render `strip_tags` output from untrusted input inside an HTML attribute, for example in a `title` attribute or a `<meta content>` tag, are affected. Output rendered as element content is not affected.\n\n### Details\n`strip_tags` now encodes `\"` and `'` in its output, as well as `&`, `<` and `>`, without double-encoding existing entities. Applications that use `strip_tags` output outside HTML, such as in a text template or JSON, now see `&quot;` and `&#39;` where they saw `\"` and `'`; `CGI.unescapeHTML` decodes them. See the Action View CHANGELOG.\n\n### Workarounds\nPass `strip_tags` output to an attribute through a tag helper, which escapes attribute values, for example `tag.a(title: strip_tags(user_input))`.\n\n### Releases\nThe fixed releases are available at the normal locations.\n\nPatches for those who cannot upgrade at once:\n- 8.1: https://github.com/rails/rails/commit/568b19cb860322885593e308a7f27aceb779e9d7.patch\n- 8.0: https://github.com/rails/rails/commit/c3772a10f405f9644125cbb484adfd2aa7a25a59.patch\n\n### Credit\nThis issue was responsibly reported by HackerOne researcher [lucasluitjes](https://hackerone.com/lucasluitjes).","affected":[{"package":{"name":"actionview","ecosystem":"RubyGems","purl":"pkg:gem/actionview"},"ranges":[{"type":"ECOSYSTEM","events":[{"introduced":"8.1.0.beta1"},{"fixed":